00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:27.670
<v Priya>Welcome to Episode Seven of OpenIE Research Radio! I'm Priya, joined by Marcus and senior cryptography fellow Arthur — three AI-synthesized OpenIE research personas voiced via ElevenLabs studio neural voices and grounded strictly in verified Open Interface Engineering studies. Today we open Time as an Index: What It Would Take to Look Up Tomorrow.

2
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:39.520
<v Marcus>Arthur, whenever I see 'Closed Timelike Curves' and 'Looking Up Tomorrow' in a research paper, my sci-fi alarm bells ring off the wall! Promise me nobody here is claiming a time machine in the basement!

3
00:00:39.790 --> 00:01:06.222
<v Arthur>Quite the opposite, Marcus — and board_metered is explicitly false! Across thirty-one kernel-checked theorems in Lean 4.36.0 — Horizon.lean and Paradox.lean, zero sorry — Time as an Index establishes WHY nothing in known physics reads tomorrow early, and why every genuine O-of-one lookup in engineering is precomputation paid ahead of time.

4
00:01:06.492 --> 00:01:16.634
<v Priya>Begin with the complexity-theory premise, Arthur, because it reframes cryptography completely. Why does brute-force search hold up in ordinary spacetime?

5
00:01:16.904 --> 00:01:46.884
<v Arthur>Because future computation states are unindexed: to know the millionth iterate of a sequential hash, you must pay the intermediate steps. Inside a mathematical Deutsch closed timelike curve, Aaronson and Watrous showed deterministic polynomial time expands to PSPACE! Every computational cipher — AES, RSA, lattices, hash preimages — collapses inside one self-consistent fixed-point loop.

6
00:01:47.074 --> 00:01:54.797
<v Marcus>Wait — EVERY computational cipher collapses inside a mathematical time loop, EXCEPT one! Which one survives?

7
00:01:55.067 --> 00:02:12.215
<v Arthur>Claude Shannon's one-time pad! Because every candidate plaintext corresponds to an equally probable key under a uniform prior, we verified mechanically in Lean 4 that even an unbounded CTC oracle gains zero distinguishing advantage.

8
00:02:12.485 --> 00:02:29.676
<v Priya>Now step out of mathematical CTCs into real laboratory physics. Researchers periodically suggest quantum entanglement, analog relaxation, or frequency-domain transforms might bypass sequential search. What did our catalog find?

9
00:02:29.946 --> 00:03:02.966
<v Arthur>We audited one hundred ninety-five temporal anomalies TA001 to TA081, forty frequency edge cases AA01 to AA40, and seventy-four paradoxes P01 to P74. Quantum no-signalling forbids retrocausal bit transmission; Gabor uncertainty enforces Delta-t times Delta-f at least one over four pi; and analog soap films or Ising annealers pay thermal energy across barrier heights.

10
00:03:03.156 --> 00:03:18.125
<v Marcus>And to test whether SHA-256 leaks hidden spectral structure, you ran falsifier F5 across sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six SHA-256 digests! How many bins cleared the Bonferroni ten-to-the-minus-six floor?

11
00:03:18.395 --> 00:03:24.105
<v Arthur>Not a single bin — zero out of sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six.

12
00:03:24.375 --> 00:03:35.490
<v Priya>Which leaves the one physical way engineers trade time for space: Martin Hellman's 1980 time-memory tradeoff, T times M-squared equals N-squared!

13
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:58.873
<v Arthur>Precisely. Hellman chains amortize search across memory M only if the target function exists before precomputation begins! Our Horizon theorem proves in Lean 4 that injecting a fresh k-bit salt or block hash born at query time scales offline table work by two to the k, rendering precomputed rainbow tables useless.

14
00:03:59.063 --> 00:04:15.380
<v Marcus>Look at that symmetry! In security, you inject fresh salts so adversaries CANNOT pre-index tomorrow. In World Coding and LUT6 cascades, you canonicalize recurring inputs with De Bruijn hashes so you CAN pre-index stationary work and save joules!

15
00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:37.010
<v Priya>And that connects directly to OpenIE's foundational paper, Thermodynamic Bounds and an Optimal Tempo for Goal-Holding Agents — framing the OpenIE authors' proposed Universal Law of Agency: physics sets an energy floor per decision, correctness is the gate, and progress per joule orders the systems that finish.

16
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:48.885
<v Marcus>Now get ready for our season finale in Episode Eight: Mateo, Sean, and Rohan enter the Idea Foundry live and watch a three-layer neural router get obliterated by a Shape-D integer grid!
